्रकार हैं—धर्म पालन, सत्य भाषण, तपस्या द्वारा इन्द्रिय निग्रह, ईर्ष्या से\nरहित होना, बुद्धिमान होना, सहिष्णु होना, शत्रु न बनाना, यज्ञ करना, दान देना, स्थिर रहना, वेदाध्ययन में\nपारंगत होना तथा व्रत पालना।*",
      "textEn": "340\n\nI believe—I believe; wealth—property; noble—royal family; beauty—beauty; penance—penance; knowledge—knowledge gained from Vedic study; vigor—strength of the senses; radiance—radiance; influence—influence; strength—physical strength; virility—enterprise; intellect—intellect;\nYoga—yoga power; not—not; to worship—to please; indeed—indeed; they become—they are; of others—of the divine; men—\nGod; with devotion—with only devotion; pleased—was pleased; God—God; elephant—elephant—foot—king of elephants (Gajendra)\nfor the sake of. Prahlad Maharaj further said: Even if a man has wealth, a royal family, beauty,\npenance, education, skill, radiance, influence, physical strength, enterprise, intelligence, and yogic power,\nbut in my opinion, no one can please God even with all these abilities. But with devotion, he can do that. Gajendra did this, and in this way, God became pleased with him.\nBecame pleased. Meaning: No physical qualification can be a means of pleasing God.\nAs it is said in the Bhagavad Gita, God can only be known through devotion (bhaktiṁ māmabhigājanaṁ). Until\nGod is pleased with the service of the devotee, he does not allow himself to be revealed (Naham\nPrakashah Sarvasya Yogamayasamavritah). This is the opinion of all the scriptures. No one can go to God through contemplation, nor through abilities, or understand them. Viprada, with sixfold qualities, the lotus-eyed one,\nThe lotus-eyed one, the one who is not separated from the lotus-eyed one, is superior. By the mind, thus, the mind is not pleased, therefore,\nHe who does not live for the sake of his family, is not respected. 10>. Meaning of the word:\n\nViprat—compared to a Brahmin; Dvishat-gunayutaat—endowed with twelve Brahmin qualities; Aravinda-nabha—Lord, Vishnu, from whose navel a lotus has emerged; Pad-aravinda—of the lotus feet; Vimukhat—turned away from devotion; Shwa-pacham—born in the lower caste or\nChandala; Vrishtam—extremely glorious; Manye—I believe; Tat-Arpit—attained refuge in the lotus feet of God; Manah—one's own\nMind; word—word; benefit—every effort; wealth—property; life—and life; purifies—purifies; he—that (devotee);\nfamily—one's own family; not—not; but—but; abundant—great; think—think; falsely—falsely; consider—consider; to think—to think. Footnote Starts Here:\n\nThe twelve qualities of a complete Brahmin are as follows—Following Dharma, speaking the truth, controlling the senses through penance, being free from envy, being intelligent, being tolerant, not making enemies, performing Yagya (sacrifice), giving charity, remaining stable, being proficient in Veda study,\nand observing vows.*"
