      "textEn": "341\n\nFootnote Ends Here.\n\nIf a Brahmin has all twelve qualifications (mentioned in the Sansatsujat Granth), but if he\nis not a devotee and is averse to the lotus feet of God, then he is even lower than that Chandal devotee\nwho has dedicated his all - mind, word, deed, property, and life - to God. Such a devotee is superior to that Brahmin because a devotee can purify his entire family,\nwhereas a so-called Brahmin with false prestige cannot purify himself. Meaning: This statement, which explains the difference between a devotee and a Karmakandi Brahmin, is from one Prahlad Maharaj, one of the twelve Mahajans. Human society is divided into four castes and four ashrams, but the main\nprinciple is to become a pure devotee of the highest order. It is said in Haribhakti-Sudhodaya—\n\nBhagavadbhakti-hina-asya-jatiha: Shastram Japah: Tapah.\nThe body is the ornament of the whole world. “If a Brahmin, Kshatriya, or Vaishya is born in a high family, but if he is not a devotee of God,\nthen all the qualities of his Brahmin, Kshatriya, or Vaishya are in vain. Undoubtedly, they are considered as the adornment of the corpse.”\n\nIn this verse, Prahlada Maharaj speaks of the Brahmins, that is, the learned Brahmins. Among all four varnas—Brahmin,\nKshatriya, Vaishya, and Shudra—the Vipra is considered the best, but one born in a lowly Chandal family\nA devotee is superior to such Vipras—there is no question of Kshatriyas, Vaishyas, etc. A devotee is superior to all others,\nbecause he always remains on the divine position, on the divine position—\n\nMother, who serves with unbroken devotion. Those who are in the state of being qualified, are imagined as Brahman. “He who remains engaged in complete devotion, who is not deviated in any circumstance, he immediately\ntranscends material qualities and attains the state of Brahman” (Bhagavad Gita 14.26).\nIn the book Sanskrit grammar, the twelve qualities of the first Kotiya Brahmin are described. They are as follows—"
