      "textEn": "343\n\nGod is always self-content, therefore when he is offered a gift,\nBy the grace of God, this gift is only for the benefit of the devotee, because God does not need anyone's service. For example, if someone's face is adorned, then in the mirror, the reflection of their face\nalso appears adorned. Meaning: In Bhakti Yoga, the devotee has to follow nine principles. This is—Listening, chanting,\nRemembrance of Vishnu, service at his feet. Archanam vandanam dasyam sakhyam atmavendanam. Through listening, chanting, etc.,\nthe praise of God is not for the benefit of God, it is for the benefit of the devotee. God is\nalways glorious, whether the devotee sings praises or not. But if a devotee sings the glory of God,\nthen the devotee himself becomes self-successful (Chetodarpanaamarjanam bhavamahadavagnirvapanam).\nBy constantly praising God, the inner self of the living being becomes pure, which enables them to understand\nthat they are not worldly, but the soul, whose real task is to advance the Krishna consciousness,\nso that they can be freed from worldly bondage. In this way, the burning fire of the world is extinguished.\n(Bhavamahadavagnirvapanam). A foolish person is greatly surprised when Krishna commands—\nSarvadharmam parityajya mamekam sharanam vraj—Give up all religious activities and come to my refuge.\nCome. Some foolish pundits even go so far as to suggest that such a demand is unreasonable. But this demand is not made for the benefit of God,\nIt is for the welfare of human society. If all humans,\nindividually and collectively, keep every object in Krishna consciousness and dedicate it to God,\nthen the entire human society will benefit from it. The person who does not offer everything to God,\nis called Avidush or Durit in this verse.\nIn the Bhagavad Gita (7.15), the Lord says in this way—\nThey do not attain the best of men, even by following the wicked and foolish. Maya-prapta-jnana asuram bhavam-ashritah. “Such wicked people are utterly foolish, they are vile among men, whose knowledge has been plundered by delusion,\nand who are like demons, of an atheistic nature; they do not come to my refuge.\"Ignorance and misfortune"
