      "textEn": "357\n\nMeaning: The cycle of physical sufferings is also the creation of God, but He himself is not under the control of physical power.\nHe is the regulator of physical power. While we are all under their thumb. When\nwe abandon our natural state (jivera 'swarupa' haye—krishnera 'nitya-dasa') then God\nproduces this material power over which all the bound souls are subject. They are supreme, and they alone can liberate the individual soul from the association of material nature.\n(They alone, having attained Me, are able to cross over this Maya). Bahiranga\nShakti, that is, Maya, imposes three types of sufferings on bound souls. Therefore, in the previous verse, Prahlad\nMaharaj prayed to God, \"No one else can protect me except you.\"\" He\nAlso said that the child's protector, his parents, cannot save him from the cycle of birth and death.\nNeither medicine nor physicians can save him from death.\nSimilarly, boats or other such means cannot save a person drowning in water, because the regulator of every object is God. Therefore, the afflicted humanity should seek refuge in Krishna, because in the final teaching of the Bhagavad Gita (18.66),\nKrishna himself asks—\n\nAbandon all Dharmas and take refuge in Me alone. I will liberate you from all sins, do not grieve. “You renounce all your religions and come to my refuge. I will rid you of all your sins. You\ndon't have to be scared.” The entire society should take advantage of this declaration and be protected by Krishna from the fear of being crushed by the past, present, and future\ncycles of time. The word ‘Nishprabhavam’ (fallen) is very significant. Every living being, in reality, keeps grinding in this physical\nworld again and again, and to escape from this state, he should take refuge in God.\nShould want. Only then will he be able to be happy. The word 'Prapannam' is also very important because without completely surrendering to the refuge of God, no one can escape.\nThe government punishes